MANIFOLD finance_manifold VERSION 1
# ── THE MANUAL'S OWN VOCABULARY, and nothing else ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This fixture exists so a plan-time refusal can mean only ONE thing: the shipped planner rejects the
# FORM the Manual documents. Planned against a fixture that merely resembled the Manual's schema,
# every `unknown column` would be ambiguous between "the Manual is wrong" and "my fixture is thin" —
# and the second reading is always available, so the finding would never be safe to act on. Every
# name the Manual's finance examples reference is declared here, at the grain its prose states.

UNIVERSE sales     = transaction BASIS events
UNIVERSE inventory = store * day  BASIS spine
# The face drivers live HERE, not on `sales`: adjudication refuses an ASSIGN/ALLOC driver drawn from
# an events universe, because a raw events measure has no single value per member to rank or weight.
# A driver must be a SPINE at the frontier grain — the (product, category) membership itself.
UNIVERSE membership = product * category BASIS spine

LEVEL transaction = txn_id      BASE
LEVEL store       = store_id    BASE
LEVEL customer    = customer_id
LEVEL region      = region
LEVEL product     = product_id  BASE
LEVEL category    = category_id
LEVEL day         = day
LEVEL date        = txn_date
LEVEL month       = month
LEVEL year        = year

HIERARCHY customers {
    transaction -> customer VIA txns(txn_id, customer_id) ;
    customer    -> region   VIA customers(customer_id, region)
}
HIERARCHY geography {
    store -> region VIA stores(store_id, region)
}
HIERARCHY products {
    transaction -> product VIA txns(txn_id, product_id)
}
# §5.6's relationship, copied from the Manual's OWN declaration in that section — same face names
# (`touch`/`assign`/`alloc`) and same drivers, so `AT {category.touch}` addresses the thing the
# Manual says it addresses. Faces are AUTHOR-NAMED, so a fixture that named them anything else would
# make the Manual's three examples unaddressable for a reason that has nothing to do with the Manual.
RELATE product <-> category VIA product_categories(product_id, category_id)
    FACES {
        touch  = TOUCH -- "revenue reaches every category a product sits in — multi-counted; totals exceed the grand total"
        assign = ASSIGN BY category_rank ORDER MIN -- "revenue lands in the product's single primary category; the rest disclosed as the shadow"
        alloc  = ALLOC BY category_weight -- "revenue splits across a product's categories by weight; totals reconcile to the grand total"
    }
    NOTE "a product belongs to up to 3 categories"
HIERARCHY calendar {
    transaction -> day   VIA txns(txn_id, day) ;
    transaction -> date  VIA txns(txn_id, txn_date) ;
    day         -> month VIA calendar(day, month) ;
    month       -> year  VIA calendar(month, year)
}

# The DEFAULT-FAMILY sugar of Chapter 3.1 needs a default to exist: `SELECT revenue AT {customer}`
# desugars to `sum(revenue @ {transaction})` only because `sum` IS revenue's family. The AS-form
# declares exactly that single-member family, which is what the Manual's 6.1 note assumes in words
# ("assuming `sum` is revenue's default family"). Reducers the examples apply explicitly — `max`,
# `avg`, `count` — reach the column through the INLINE reduction path and need no family membership.
MEASURE revenue ON sales FROM txns AS sum(amount)
MEASURE cost    ON sales FROM txns AS sum(cost_amount)
MEASURE orders  ON sales FROM txns AS count(*)

# §2.5's juxtaposition example puts `revenue` and `level.last` in ONE frame at {region}: two
# populations, side by side, each honest about where it comes from. That needs a SECOND universe
# carrying a stock, and a `last` member for the position reading.
# `aov` is DERIVED, exactly as the Manual's 6.6 ratio idiom builds it — and that is also what makes
# 2.3's clarify reachable. A derived ratio has no single root grain, so `avg(aov)` with no pin has
# more than one candidate input grain to be ambiguous BETWEEN. Declared as a base measure it would
# have exactly one candidate, `avg(aov)` would quietly serve, and the gate would report the Manual's
# documented clarify as a contradiction when the only thing wrong was the fixture.
DERIVED aov = revenue / orders

MEASURE category_rank   ON membership FROM product_categories AS min(rank)
MEASURE category_weight ON membership FROM product_categories AS sum(weight)

MEASURE level ON inventory FROM inv VALUE units
    FAMILY {
        last ORDER day
        max
        min
        count
    }
